{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2}={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}-2x{y}^{′}-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=2,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =1-4-3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+(-3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333653" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y=3{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0+3}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+3=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333622" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y={x}^{2},\\,y(2)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-6y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(2)={y}^{′}(2)=2.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(2)=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2},\\, & z(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(2)=-3$ and $z(2)=2,$ we place $y=-3$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xz-6y+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-3)+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4+18+4 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $26$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =26\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+26=28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ |  |  | $28$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333548" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333548" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00