6ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333654" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=z(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2}=4x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2} & =4x \\\\ 3y{y}^{″} & =4x+({y}^{′})^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x+({y}^{′})^{2}}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{8}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333822" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′}=2x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{4})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})=z(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-332989" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′}=3x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′} & =3x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3x-2y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3x-2y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3x-2yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-1)+5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈0$\nand ${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333724" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈1$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333817" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x=0,\\,y(-1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x & =0 \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =x-3{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x-3{y}^{′}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(-1)={y}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(-1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y},\\, & z(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{-1-3(-2)}{1}=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot 1=5 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{0-3\\cdot 3}{(-1)}=9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+9=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $9$ | $9$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(1)=z(1)≈12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-334003" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2y-x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2y-x{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{18}=\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{25}{9}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{25}{9}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{15}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{13}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333875" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{25}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x{y}^{′}-y}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1-(-2)}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(3/2)\\cdot (5/2)-(-3/2)}{(-3/2)^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{7}{6}=\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{11}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{11}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333374" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+2y & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =4{y}^{′}-2y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{y}^{′}-2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4z-2y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4z-2y}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4z-2y}{x}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{1}=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=7 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4z-2y}{x}=\\frac{4\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 0}{2}=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot 1=14 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+7=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=7+14=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $7$ | $7$ | $14$ | $14$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $21$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "21"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333994" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ 2x{y}^{″} & =-5({y}^{′})^{2}-x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{5({y}^{′})^{2}+x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=-1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{2}+1}{2\\cdot 1}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-3\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1-3=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)^{2}+2}{2\\cdot 2}=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-4\\cdot 1=-4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{41}{2}\\cdot 1=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-4=-3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-4-\\frac{41}{2}=-\\frac{49}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $-\\frac{49}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈-\\frac{49}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43d34ed04a93620e3d770f922742a8a43c7a98a5ea2b2f7de5eb62c354ef1ad0"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{25}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{41}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{49}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "q-333997" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{49}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/state.json" "file_sha256" "8940d0b437ea130d1c8c364649b9a377aa4c718656ac00eb25b79a79158e384d"} {"question" "e-21664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 4xy' - 3y = 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(0) = -2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y'$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nUse the information above to approximate the value of $y(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/example-21664.json" "file_sha256" "e0d9da08084bf5040a67c72239831627a0ee689749f309d20ed1868cb6bf4df5"} {"question" "e-21664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-3y & =2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-4x{y}^{′}+3y+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = -4xz + 3y + 2x.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0) = y'(0) = -2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(0)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-4xz+3y+2x,\\, & z(0)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=-2,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-4xz+3y+2x \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0+3+0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-2)=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx -1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/example-21664.json" "file_sha256" "e0d9da08084bf5040a67c72239831627a0ee689749f309d20ed1868cb6bf4df5"} {"question" "e-21668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx^2y'' + 2y' = 4x^2, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\qquad y'(1) = -1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/example-21668.json" "file_sha256" "b40939d65ee3ed35c4bd6c67f49ed90ea4bced49d30ed44ac5ffddd66346a79d"} {"question" "e-21668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1) = y'(1) = -1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at $x=\\dfrac32,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32$\nand\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/example-21668.json" "file_sha256" "b40939d65ee3ed35c4bd6c67f49ed90ea4bced49d30ed44ac5ffddd66346a79d"} {"question" "e-21669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n2x y'' - (y')^2 + 4y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and $y'(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/example-21669.json" "file_sha256" "146ed7dea84abccc400ac9ea35be442912eb551a36cb23fa0a6124ba61713572"} {"question" "e-21669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ 2x{y}^{″} & =({y}^{′})^{2}-4y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({y}^{′})^{2}-4y}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y',$ $z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{z^2 - 4y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1) = y'(1) = 2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z=y'$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-4(-1)}{2\\cdot 1}=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+2=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=6 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x}=\\frac{{6}^{2}-4\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot 2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=6+8=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ | $6$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $14$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 7$\nand\n$y'(3) = z(3) \\approx 14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070918/example-21669.json" "file_sha256" "146ed7dea84abccc400ac9ea35be442912eb551a36cb23fa0a6124ba61713572"}] "usage" {"e-21669" [] "q-333374" [] "q-332989" [] "e-21664" [] "q-333724" [] "e-21668" []} "basis" 2526 "retractions" [[17592186221893 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$xy^{″} + 3yy^{′} = 2x, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) =-2$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{4})$ and $y^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})$.$y(\\frac{5}{4}) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$y^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"approximating-the-solution-to-a-second-order-ode-using-eulers-method-with-two-steps\"></a>"] [17592186221928 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$xy^{″} + 2yy^{′} = 3x, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) =-1$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $y^{′}(2)$.$y(2) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$y^{′}(2) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186221898 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0, y(1) =-2, y^{′}(1) = 1$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and $y^{′}(2)$.$-1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [17592186221114 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y'' + 4xy' - 3y = 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(0) = -2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y'$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nUse the information above to approximate the value of $y(1)$."] [17592186221114 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} + 4xy^{′} - 3y &= 2x \\\\\ny^{″} &= -4xy^{′} + 3y + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable\n\n$$\nz = y', z' = y''\n$$\n\nin the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = -4xz + 3y + 2x\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(0) = y'(0) = -2\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 1, \\\\ z^{′} =-4xz + 3y + 2x,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) =-2\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=-2$, we place $y=1$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z =-2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= -4xz + 3y + 2x \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 0 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + (-2) =-1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-2 + 3 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx -1\n$$"] [17592186221117 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$x^2y'' + 2y' = 4x^2, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\qquad y'(1) = -1$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$."] [17592186221117 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} + 2y^{′} &= 4x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2}y^{″} &= 4x^{2} - 2y^{′} \\\\\ny^{″} &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 2y^{′}}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable\n\n$$\nz = y', z' = y''\n$$\n\nin the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(1) = y'(1) = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1, \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{4x^{2} - 2z}{x^{2}},\\end{vmatrix}z(1) =-1\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z =-1 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 2z}{x^{2}} \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot 1^{2} - 2 \\cdot (-1))/(1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 2}{1} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy =-1 + (-\\frac{1}{2}) =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-1 + 3 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2\n$$"] [17592186221118 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$2x y'' - (y')^2 + 4y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 2$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and $y'(3)$."] [17592186221118 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z)\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2xy^{″} - (y^{′})^{2} + 4y &= 0 \\\\\n2xy^{″} &= (y^{′})^{2} - 4y \\\\\ny^{″} &= ((y^{′})^{2} - 4y)/(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y'$, $z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{z^2 - 4y}{2x}\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n\n$$\nz(1) = y'(1) = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1, \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x},\\end{vmatrix}z(1) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z=y'$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z = 2 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x} = (2^{2} - 4(-1))/(2 \\cdot 1) = 4 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy =-1 + 2 = 1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 2 + 4 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y$, $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new}$, $y_\\text{new}$, and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= z = 6 \\\\\nz^{′} &= \\frac{z^{2} - 4y}{2x} = \\frac{6^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 2} = 8 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 6 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx = 8 \\cdot 1 = 8 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 6 = 7 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz = 6 + 8 = 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ | $6$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $14$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(3) \\approx 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny'(3) = z(3) \\approx 14\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220329 17592186220330 17592186220331 17592186220333 17592186220334 17592186220335] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220310 :answer-field/id] [17592186220310 :answer-field/key] [17592186220310 :answer-field/type] [17592186220311 :answer-field/id] [17592186220311 :answer-field/key] [17592186220311 :answer-field/type] [17592186220328 :answer-field/id] [17592186220328 :answer-field/key] [17592186220328 :answer-field/type] [17592186220332 :answer-field/id] [17592186220332 :answer-field/key] [17592186220332 :answer-field/type] [17592186220542 :answer-field/id] [17592186220542 :answer-field/key] [17592186220542 :answer-field/type] [17592186220543 :answer-field/id] [17592186220543 :answer-field/key] [17592186220543 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
